					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/syka-bee" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(438108); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=438108&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608954167" alt="Syka Bee" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=438108)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/syka-bee" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(438108); return false;" >Syka Bee</a></span> <span class="odate time_1347981621 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Sep 2012 15:20</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1567553">
									

<p>There is not one kind of &quot;cell tower&quot;. There are a myriad of different systems - &quot;GSM&quot; can colloquially refer to HSPA and WCDMA sorts of networks, but it's more correctly used to describe 2G EDGE-style networks. Additionally, there's several manufacturers of equipment of this - and that ranges from dumber kit from the early 00s to the higher tech stuff in the new towers - a virus can't just 'hack' random ones and transfer itself - since not every tower will have the same kit powering it. Unless the virus is made by a team of experts that have kit on hand and have vulnerabilities for each&#8230; not going to happen.</p>
<p>Additionally - &quot;Upon the completion of a SCP-1558 intercepted call, a recorded audio file of said call is downloaded to SCP-1558-2’s hard drive, regardless of Internet connection or power status.&quot; This here is enough for a downvote. If it can't contact the tower via the internet, it still works. If it's off, it still works. Because somehow this radio tower virus can use <em>the powers of magic</em> to arrange magnetic bits on a powered off hard drive to form the data that makes up an encoded sound file, then via the same MoIP (Magic over IP) connection, update the file allocation table of the HDD so that the file 'exists' to the OS. Note that files aren't stored sequentially - they are quite commonly fragmented (see something I made yonks ago that explains it simply <a href="http://reddrgn.net/tidbits/files/howdefragworks.png">here</a>). So to work around that, you would need to have the <em>contents</em> of the HDD right there to read off and work - which is just impossible, as that's just &quot;it happens because it happens and it's *spooky* oooh, disregarding the fact that <em>this isn't how servers or viruses work</em>.</p>

							</div>

								
					
